>possible" in one direction, the old amiga version had
><ctrl>direction, which would send you "as far as possible, until
>something interesting".  This was handy, because if you had
>
>
>		####@################
>			#
>			#####
>
>you could type <ctrl><right> and it would stop you at the fork in the tunnel.
>
>These are small things, but for me they were very handy, and now that they're
>gone, their absence is annoying.
>
>Where these features never incorporated into the base code?
>

The "standard" way of doing that is "M(direction)".

All the stuff you want is available in the base code, however accessing
it varies considerably from machine to machine.  I personally think it
would be neat if nethack supported a way to map commands to new keys 
within the game.  e.g.: 
 - You type #keymap
 - nethack prompts you: "enter the old command:"
 - You type "M4" i.e. Run west
 - nethack prompts you: "enter the new keymap:"
 - You type ctrl-4 (on the numeric keypad)
 - nethack then figures out what that is, and saves it in some config file.

That way, on a keyboard (like mine) with a keypad and four cursor movement
keys, I could easily map the cursor movement keys to be "run-dir", and the
numeric keypad to be "walk-dir".

I might even implement this some day, however the one time I looked at 
nethack keymappings (to change P/W and T/R to work for both rings and armor),
I quickly decided it was too complicated, and forgot about it.  One of these
days...
